    "body": "Great on any Friday: WOLFBOX MV24 4000A - Car Jump Starter with 65W Quick Charger\nAmazon Autos UK Going Live in 2027, but Dealers Still Get the Last Word\n7 photos\nPhoto: Amazon\nAs you're well aware, Amazon is a completely different animal from the company's virtual bookstore and distributor-back retail model from the 1990s. The Washington-based colossus has become very good at removing people from the buying process, be it groceries, car parts, or actual cars. Buying a vehicle through Amazon Autos in Britain, however, will still require a trip to the dealer.\nThe company founded by Jeff Bezos in Bellevue more than three decades ago is bringing its online car marketplace to the United Kingdom in 2027, yet a few steps remain firmly in dealer territory. Paperwork, final sale, and handover, for example, will be handled by dealers throughout the United Kingdom.\nAmazon Autos UK's debut sounds less revolutionary than the marketing suggests, but nevertheless, Amazon Autos could become a disruptor in this part of the world. The American corporation wants to control the part of the transaction that matters the most for many customers, specifically when the customer starts searching with the purpose of deciding which model is worth their hard-earned money.\nSouth Korean automaker Hyundai is the first confirmed brand. Amazon's partner will kick things off in the United Kingdom with a selection of electric and internal combustion vehicles, including the new Ioniq 3 , the larger Ioniq 5, the Kona, and the i20. Used vehicles wearing the Hyundai badge will also be featured, while Vertu Motors will supply additional used vehicle listings.\nMore automakers and retailers will be announced before the platform goes live in the first part of 2027. The shopping process will be pretty familiar to anyone who has bought something from Amazon. For starters, customers have search tools that allow them to search by model, trim, and paint color. They can also view upfront prices, available stock, and obtain an indicative valuation of their old car.\nPhoto: Amazon Naturally, the platform allows them to investigate personal contract purchase and hire purchase finance options. After reserving the vehicle of their liking, the customer has to contact the chosen dealer to complete the purchase in person. Retailers are the ones who set the final price and retain the customer relationship, and Amazon cannot do anything about it, even though it would like to.\nThink of Amazon Autos UK as the showroom before the showroom, if that makes sense. Amazon wants prospective customers to do their research using its tools, narrow down their choices, and hit the dealer knowing which car they intend to purchase. Considering that online inquiries sometimes involve buyers who are still months away from buying anything, that could be useful for retailers.\nCustomers who already made their minds in regard to what car and finance option they want is a valuable lead. More valuable than someone asking over the phone whether a particular model comes in blue. This arrangement, however, also reveals the limits of Amazon's ambitions in the UK's online car market, which is led by Autotrader.\nBritain's established online automotive marketplace has a ginormous advantage in inventory and dealer relations. Autotrader's latest half-year report shows around 13,900 retailer forecourts advertising on the platform, but nevertheless, Amazon is the much bigger corporation that would surely win a war of attrition.\nFrom the looks of it, Amazon's initial focus in the UK is less about persuading car buyers to use its platform in lieu of Autotrader. Instead, the American newcomer is likely using its strengths to convince dealers to view its platform as a viable option.\nVia // Amazon\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAmazon Hyundai UK business industry\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tourne Cruise Edition Is a European All-Rounder Camper Van Built for All-Season Travel",
